Ports upstream PR vllm-project/vllm#61612 (open) to this pin.

Since vllm #57214, FlashInfer's metadata builder skips its blocking seq_lens.cpu() for models without speculative
decoding and plans from seq_lens_cpu_upper_bound instead. With PP=2 under concurrent load the server then dies with an
illegal memory access on PP0 (#328). GPU core dumps of three such crashes (two on 0.31.0, one on 0.32.0) all name
flashinfer::PersistentVariableLengthMergeStatesKernel on device 0.

Why, as read from the code (vllm #61239 and #61612; not measured here): the skipped call was also the host
barrier between steps. With async scheduling the CPU can then plan step N+1 while step N's plan is still being copied
to the GPU: plan() writes every index array into one pinned host buffer per wrapper and enqueues a single
cudaMemcpyAsync from it, so N+1's plan can overwrite those bytes before N's copy reads them, and the merge kernel reads
indptrs that do not belong to its step.

The PR keeps the shortcut only when the scheduler is synchronous.

Measured on 0.32.0 with the batch launcher at #328's settings (PP=2 at 32,32, fp8 KV, 64 seqs, MAX_LEN 340000, prefix
cache, vision with offload, int8 prefill attention, GPU_UTIL 0.94, async scheduling on) and 24 concurrent agent
conversations whose prompts grow to ~50k tokens, some with images, on two rented machines with two RTX 3090s each:

  unpatched, machine 1 (EPYC 7543, driver 595.84)           PP0's worker dies at 105.7 minutes; the core dump
                                                             names the merge kernel above
  shortcut disabled by hand, machine 1                      240 minutes, 3,156 requests ok, no 500s, no crash
  this patch, machine 2 (Threadripper 3975WX, driver 595.99) 60 minutes, 707 requests ok, no 500s, no crash
  unpatched, machine 2                                       60 minutes, 781 requests ok, no 500s, no crash

So the evidence that this cut fixes 0.32.0 is machine 1's pair, with the shortcut forced off by hand rather than this
exact code: one crash at 105.7 minutes unpatched against 240 clean minutes, one boot each. Machine 2 crashed 0.31.0
unpatched at 8.0 and 27.4 minutes but ran 0.32.0 unpatched for an hour without a crash, so its clean hour with this
patch shows the cut boots and serves on 0.32.0, not that it removes the crash there. The code change is the same one
the 0.31.0 cut carries, where machine 2's runs do compare. The 400s in every run are the load's second-image
requests, refused by the one-image limit.

Applied unchanged: the PR's flashinfer.py hunks cherry-pick onto 0.32.0 without conflicts (its pre-commit workflow
hunk is not part of this patch).

Kind: fix
What: with async scheduling on, FlashInfer's metadata builder takes the blocking seq_lens.cpu() again, the host
  barrier between steps that vllm #57214 dropped
Upstream: vllm #61612 (open); the regression is from vllm #57214. vllm #61622 (open, for #61621) makes the
  blocking read wait on an event instead of holding the CUDA context lock, but leaves the shortcut as it is, so
  without #61612 it does not fix this
Cut-against: 0.32.0
Retires-when: the pin that carries #61612, or another change that keeps the barrier under async scheduling

--- exported from f1dfe0337 (vllm-pr61612-flashinfer-async-barrier); regenerate with scripts/export-patch.sh, do not edit ---

diff --git a/v1/attention/backends/flashinfer.py b/v1/attention/backends/flashinfer.py
index f6c3e262d..462a85215 100755
--- a/v1/attention/backends/flashinfer.py
+++ b/v1/attention/backends/flashinfer.py
@@ -748,6 +748,7 @@
         self.model_config = vllm_config.model_config
         self.attention_config = vllm_config.attention_config
         self._num_speculative_tokens = vllm_config.num_speculative_tokens
+        self._async_scheduling = vllm_config.scheduler_config.async_scheduling or False
         self._workspace_buffer = None
         self._prefill_wrapper: (
             BatchPrefillWithPagedKVCacheWrapper | BatchDCPPrefillWrapper | None
@@ -1509,9 +1510,14 @@
         if needs_seq_lens_cpu:
             if (
                 self._num_speculative_tokens == 0
+                and not self._async_scheduling
                 and common_attn_metadata.seq_lens_cpu_upper_bound is not None
             ):
                 # No speculative token accounting, so the upper bound is exact.
+                # This shortcut is safe only when the scheduler is synchronous:
+                # with async scheduling the CPU may plan step N+1 while step N's
+                # pinned workspace copy is still in flight, causing the GPU to
+                # read a step's plan data. See #61239.
                 seq_lens_cpu = common_attn_metadata.seq_lens_cpu_upper_bound
             else:
                 with gpu_sync_allowed():
